  - id: TRAIN-SOCO-TEAM
    category: 0
    type: 0
    speaker: Professor
    text: In this simulation, you will practice several crime-scene activities yourself.
      In actual operations, these responsibilities may be divided among trained members
      of the crime-scene team, including photography, evidence recording, collection
      and specialized examination.
    duration: 11
    priority: 1
    skippable: 1
    replaceBehavior: 3
    notes: Once per session at a completed training milestone; generic principles,
      no case answers or assessment contribution.
  - id: TRAIN-PHOTOGRAPHY
    category: 1
    type: 0
    speaker: Professor
    text: Crime-scene photography preserves how the scene looked before anything
      is moved, including the original position and condition of evidence. The photographer
      documents the overall scene and important details; in actual practice, as many
      photographs as reasonably necessary may be taken. This simulation uses route
      points to practice that documentation.
    duration: 13
    priority: 1
    skippable: 1
    replaceBehavior: 3
    notes: Once per session at a completed training milestone; generic principles,
      no case answers or assessment contribution.
  - id: TRAIN-MARKER
    category: 6
    type: 0
    speaker: Professor
    text: Evidence markers help the team identify the same item across photographs,
      notes and evidence records. They support consistent documentation, but do not
      replace it.
    duration: 8
    priority: 1
    skippable: 1
    replaceBehavior: 3
    notes: Once per session at a completed training milestone; generic principles,
      no case answers or assessment contribution.
  - id: TRAIN-TRACE-SOURCE
    category: 3
    type: 0
    speaker: Professor
    text: The knife is physical evidence, while blood on it is material that may
      need its own sample and record. Separately identifying the object and the material
      collected from it preserves where each finding came from. Here, the additional
      trace marker helps you practice that source identification.
    duration: 12
    priority: 1
    skippable: 1
    replaceBehavior: 3
    notes: Once per session at a completed training milestone; generic principles,
      no case answers or assessment contribution.
  - id: TRAIN-MEASUREMENT
    category: 7
    type: 0
    speaker: Professor
    text: Measurements preserve where an item was located in relation to the scene.
      Photographs alone may not fully capture spatial relationships; the team's measurement
      and sketch work helps reconstruct the original layout after evidence is moved.
    duration: 10
    priority: 1
    skippable: 1
    replaceBehavior: 3
    notes: Once per session at a completed training milestone; generic principles,
      no case answers or assessment contribution.
  - id: TRAIN-FINGERPRINT
    category: 9
    type: 0
    speaker: Professor
    text: Fingerprint personnel carefully develop latent prints before lifting and
      preserving them for examination. The sequence helps protect the print and the
      record of its location. A recovered fingerprint can establish contact with
      an object, but alone does not explain when or why that contact occurred.
    duration: 12
    priority: 1
    skippable: 1
    replaceBehavior: 3
    notes: Once per session at a completed training milestone; generic principles,
      no case answers or assessment contribution.
  - id: LEAD-FINGERPRINT-VENDOR
    category: 9
    type: 0
    speaker: Professor
    text: Paul's print confirms that he handled the knife at some point, but it doesn't
      tell us when he touched it or what he did with it. It isn't proof that he used
      it in the attack. Let's follow up with the vendor and ask whether he noticed
      anyone else near John's house besides Peter.
    duration: 20
    priority: 1
    skippable: 1
    replaceBehavior: 3
    notes: Once per session after the locked RC-03 fingerprint response and existing
      explanations; investigative lead only, never reveals the vendor answer or awards
      points.
  - id: TRAIN-BIOLOGICAL-SAMPLE
    category: 8
    type: 0
    speaker: Professor
    text: Biological material is placed in its own container and identified so it
      can be traced back to the exact item or area where it came from. Samples from
      different sources must remain distinguishable. Collecting a sample preserves
      material for examination; it does not by itself explain what happened.
    duration: 12
    priority: 1
    skippable: 1
    replaceBehavior: 3
    notes: Once per session at a completed training milestone; generic principles,
      no case answers or assessment contribution.
  - id: TRAIN-PACKAGING
    category: 11
    type: 0
    speaker: Professor
    text: Collection does not end when an item is picked up. The evidence collector
      properly packages, secures and identifies it so its condition and identity
      are protected during handling.
    duration: 9
    priority: 1
    skippable: 1
    replaceBehavior: 3
    notes: Once per session at a completed training milestone; generic principles,
      no case answers or assessment contribution.
  - id: TRAIN-CUSTODY
    category: 11
    type: 0
    speaker: Professor
    text: Packaged and secured evidence must remain accounted for. The evidence recorder
      or custodian records who handled it and where it went after collection. Storing
      it in this simulation practices accountability; the button press does not represent
      every real chain-of-custody step.
    duration: 11
    priority: 1
    skippable: 1
    replaceBehavior: 3
    notes: Once per session at a completed training milestone; generic principles,
      no case answers or assessment contribution.
